   "texts": {
    "base": {
     "text": " The sky looks blue during the day because air molecules scatter sunlight, and they scatter short blue wavelengths much more than longer red ones. This scattering sends blue light in all directions, so we see blue overhead. At sunset, sunlight travels through a much longer path of atmosphere, so most blue light gets scattered away before it reaches our eyes. The remaining light that reaches us is mostly red and orange, making the sky look warm-colored near the horizon. This happens because of Rayleigh scattering, which depends strongly on wavelength.",
     "sha256": "8068cf3e04c54f89f3275b0ff21bd7b6f5d1aa2b88c975f8d6cd4886dcdaf621",
     "n_chars": 556
    },
    "cand": {
     "text": " The sky looks blue during the day because air molecules scatter sunlight, and they scatter short blue wavelengths much more than long red ones. This scattering process is called Rayleigh scattering. At sunset, sunlight travels through a much longer path of atmosphere, so most of the blue light is scattered away before it reaches our eyes. The remaining light that reaches us is mostly red and orange, which is why the sky turns red at sunset.",
     "sha256": "643c873377757b460c58b8b984d2e464e2d28128583d8adb83e6f2cf5f680956",
     "n_chars": 445
    }
